<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A launchd USER AGENT, not a daemon.

  deploy/macos/install.sh is what puts this in place, and it REWRITES the
  program path below: it installs the binary into ~/.local/bin, because Full
  Disk Access is granted to a path by a person for their own account and
  nothing about this install should belong to root. The path here is the
  fallback for a hand install:

    cp us.schoenstatt.sion-backup.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/us.schoenstatt.sion-backup.plist

  An agent rather than a daemon. The original reason — the credentials sat in
  the login Keychain, which only unlocks inside a user session — no longer
  applies: nothing is cached, and every run fetches its credentials from
  Eumaeus and discards them. The reason now is Full Disk Access. Reading
  ~/Documents, ~/Desktop and Mail is gated by TCC rather than by Unix
  permissions, and the grant belongs to a user. Whatever runs this has to hold
  it: for an agent, that is a checkbox the person clicks in System Settings ›
  Privacy & Security › Full Disk Access; for a LaunchDaemon it is a PPPC
  profile pushed by MDM.

  NOTE: this was reasoned from Apple's documentation rather than from a
  machine that had taken a backup — the legacy project scoped macOS and
  shipped nothing, its "mac 0.2" folder being empty. The first Mac was
  enrolled on 12 September 2026 and found three things this file could not
  have told anybody: the release binaries are quarantined by Gatekeeper, there
  was no installer to register this agent at all, and enrolment did not keep
  the plan it was given. Treat the second Mac as a test too.

  RunAtLoad plus KeepAlive means it starts when the user logs in and is
  restarted if it exits. The scheduling is the program's own — see
  business/domain/plan for why a StartCalendarInterval would be the wrong
  answer for a laptop that is asleep at the appointed hour.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>us.schoenstatt.sion-backup</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/bin/sion-backup</string>
    <string>daemon</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>

  <!-- Both streams to one file. The program writes structured lines, so
       interleaving them loses nothing and halves the number of files somebody
       has to be talked through finding over the phone. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/sion-backup.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/tmp/sion-backup.log</string>

  <!-- Below normal, so a backup does not make the machine feel slow. -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
  <key>Nice</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
